**Can Java/Swing animate sprite sheets?** Yes, easily. The standard pattern: load the sheet once as a `BufferedImage`, then on each frame draw a rectangular sub-region with `g.drawImage(sheet, dx1, dy1, dx2, dy2, sx1, sy1, sx2, sy2, null)`. A `javax.swing.Timer` ticks ~60 times per second and advances the frame index. So one sheet = many frames = animation. You do **not** need single-image assets.

**Do your specific files work?** Mostly yes, with caveats:

- *country-platform-preview.png* — works as a background. It's small (looks like ~384×216). For 1280×720 you'll scale it ~3–4× (fine since it's pixel art) or tile it. Easy.
- *player-spritemap-v9.png* (Ars Notoria hero) — works great. Side-view idle/walk/run/jump/punch/kick/fall are all in there.
- *goblin.png* (LPC by Redshrike) — works *but* this sheet was made for **top-down** RPGs, not side-view brawlers. It has 4-directional walk cycles and attacks that look right from above. For side view you'll use only the "walk left/right" and "slash left/right" rows. It'll be passable but the goblin will read slightly "top-down tilted" next to your hero. Fine for class.
- *rogue_spritesheet_calciumtrice.png* — works. Side-view, ~32×32 frames in rows by action.
- *wolfsheet1.png* — works. Side-view wolf with multiple animation strips.

**Mixed-art warning:** hero, goblin, rogue, wolf are all from different artists with different pixel densities and palettes. They will not look stylistically unified. Totally normal for a class project.

**3-day reality check.** Your scope is ambitious but doable if we keep rules simple:
- Fixed single-screen arena (no scrolling) — you already implied this
- Simple AI: walk toward player, swing when in melee range
- Hitboxes = bounding-box overlap (no per-pixel collision)
- Treat level 4's "enemies from behind" as a **stretch goal** — get levels 1–3 rock solid first
- Your step-by-step plan (View first, then Model, then Controller, with a tester at each step) is exactly right

